.TH "LDM2" "1" "09/18/2026" "" ""
.SH "NAME"
ldm \- a display manager for LTSP
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fIldm\fR <vt[1\-N]> <:[0\-N]>
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
ldm(1) starts an X server and presents the user with a login screen, similar to the gdm(1) login prompt. instead of using the XDMCP protocol, ldm(1) uses ssh(1) connect to remote servers, then starts an Xsession either via ssh(1) X forwarding, or direct TCP/IP connection.

It was designed for the LTSP project, but could be used in a non\-ltsp environment as well.
.SH "OPTIONS"
<vt[1\-N]>
    tty where ldm will run

<:[0\-N]>
    X display that ldm uses.
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
ldm reads its settings from the environment. On an LTSP client that environment
comes from \fBlts.conf\fP(5), which is where every \fBLDM_\fR variable is
described; the ones that decide what the login screen says and how it looks are
summarised here.
.SS "What it says"
.IP "\fBLDM_USERNAME_PROMPT\fR, \fBLDM_PASSWORD_PROMPT\fR" 4
The two prompts beside the entry field. Each is Pango markup and reaches the
screen unchanged, so the value carries its own emphasis \(em the defaults are
\fB<b>Username</b>\fR and \fB<b>Password</b>\fR, translated through gettext.
.IP "\fBLDM_VERIFY_MESSAGE\fR" 4
The line shown while the password is being checked. Also Pango markup; the
default is a bold, translated "Verifying password.  Please wait.".
.IP "\fBLDM_NEWPASSWORD_PROMPT\fR, \fBLDM_NEWPASSWORD_AGAIN_PROMPT\fR" 4
The two prompts shown when a domain password has to be changed at the greeter
\(em see \fBPASSWORDS THAT MUST BE CHANGED\fR below. Pango markup as well; the
defaults are \fB<b>New password</b>\fR and \fB<b>New password again</b>\fR.
.IP "\fBLDM_NEWPASSWORD_MESSAGE\fR" 4
The line shown above those prompts, saying why they appeared. The default is
a bold, translated "This password must be changed before signing in." \(em
which is worded to cover both cases, because the domain reports a password
that ran out and one an administrator handed over as an initial password in
exactly the same way.
.IP "" 4
Setting any of these replaces the translation for that string. Leave them unset
and a localised image keeps its own wording.
.SS "How it looks"
.IP "\fBLDM_THEME\fR" 4
A directory under \fI/usr/share/ldm/themes\fR, or an absolute path. Files are
looked up in it one at a time and each one that is missing is taken from the
\fBltsp\fR theme instead \(em so a theme can consist of nothing but a
background image. The other side of that rule: a theme that does carry a
\fIgreeter.css\fR replaces the default one outright rather than adding to it.
.IP "\fBLDM_CLOCK_FORMAT\fR" 4
An \fBstrftime\fP(3) format, Pango markup allowed. Default
\fB"%Y\-%m\-%d, <b>%H:%M</b>"\fR.
.PP
Colours, fonts and spacing come from \fIgreeter.css\fR in the theme directory,
loaded as GTK3 CSS at \fBGTK_STYLE_PROVIDER_PRIORITY_APPLICATION\fR, which puts
it above the GTK theme. Five widgets carry a name so they can be addressed
separately:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
#ldm\-prompt        the Username / Password line beside the entry
#ldm\-status        the status line under it
#ldm\-host          host name and address
#ldm\-clock         the clock
#ldm\-preferences   the Preferences button
.fi
.RE
.PP
The Preferences button is in that list because it is drawn with relief
\fBNONE\fR: it has no ground of its own and sits straight on the background
image, exactly as the four labels do. A theme that lightens the labels and
forgets it leaves one word in the default near\-black.
.PP
A theme whose background is a dark photograph usually needs no more than the
following, and the shadow does more work than the colour \(em a photograph is
light in some places and dark in others:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
#ldm\-prompt, #ldm\-status, #ldm\-host, #ldm\-clock, #ldm\-preferences {
    color: #f2f2f2;
    text\-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85);
}
.fi
.RE
.SH "PASSWORDS THAT MUST BE CHANGED"
.PP
Where \fBRDP_PREAUTH\fR asks a Kerberos KDC, a password that has to be changed
can be changed at the login screen instead of merely being reported. It is the
only place a thin client can offer that: the client is not a domain member, and
the session server would only repeat the refusal.
.PP
Two situations arrive here, and the protocol does not distinguish them. One is
a password that ran out. The other is an account that never had one of the
user's own \(em an administrator set an initial password and marked it to be
replaced at first sign\-in, which is \fBpwdLastSet=0\fR in Active Directory and
the \fBREQUIRES_PWCHANGE\fR attribute on an MIT KDC. Such an account has no
expiry date at all, and the KDC still answers \fBKDC_ERR_KEY_EXPIRED\fR, which
is why the wording here says a password must be changed rather than that it
expired. Both end the same way: the user picks a password, and the attribute or
the expiry is cleared by the domain as part of the change.
.PP
The KDC will, however, still issue a ticket for the service
\fBkadmin/changepw\fR, and that exception is what makes the change possible at
all. ldm asks for the new password twice, sets it with that ticket
over the protocol from RFC 3244 (which is what Active Directory speaks), and
then signs in with the new password so the session starts without asking again.
.PP
Three things follow from how this works, and are worth knowing before enabling
it:
.IP "" 4
The rules are the domain's. Length, complexity, history and minimum age are
checked by the controller, and when it refuses, its own wording is what the
user is shown \(em it describes the rule that actually applies.
.IP "" 4
The change needs the controller holding the PDC emulator role. Signing in
survives the loss of any one controller; changing a password does not, and the
message says so rather than blaming the password.
.IP "" 4
The new password goes to the KDC and nowhere else. It reaches the session
server only afterwards, as the account's actual password, and it is wiped from
memory rather than freed.
.PP
\fBRDP_PREAUTH_CHANGE_PASSWORD=False\fR turns the offer off, leaving the
expired password reported as before. Set it where passwords are changed
somewhere else on purpose.
.PP
A KDC that does not answer is a different case and stops the login: the
credentials were never checked by anyone, and starting a session with them
would land the user in xrdp's own dialog with no way back \(em the dead end
this check exists to avoid. The greeter says that the authentication server
cannot be reached and that the password is not the problem.
.SH "FILES"
.IP "\fI/usr/share/ldm/themes/\fR" 4
The themes. \fBltsp\fR is the default and the fallback for every file another
theme does not carry.
.IP "\fI/var/log/ldm.log\fR" 4
Unless \fBLDM_SYSLOG\fR sends it elsewhere. On a thin client this lives in RAM
and dies with the session.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBlts.conf\fP(5), \fBstrftime\fP(3), \fBssh\fP(1)
